1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:06,018
Breathing and gas exchange.

2
00:00:06,018 --> 00:00:09,053
Right now, without a single thought, you

3
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:11,616
breathe in, and you breathe out.

4
00:00:11,616 --> 00:00:14,203
So what actually happens inside the chest

5
00:00:14,203 --> 00:00:15,462
with every breath?

6
00:00:15,462 --> 00:00:16,694
Let's find out!

7
00:00:18,433 --> 00:00:21,124
Let's follow one breath down the trachea,

8
00:00:21,124 --> 00:00:23,972
the windpipe! Air enters through the nose

9
00:00:23,972 --> 00:00:26,596
or mouth, passes the pharynx and larynx,

10
00:00:26,596 --> 00:00:28,429
and drops into this tube.

11
00:00:28,429 --> 00:00:31,291
C-shaped rings of cartilage hold it open.

12
00:00:31,291 --> 00:00:33,915
At its base, the trachea divides into the

13
00:00:33,915 --> 00:00:36,049
right and left primary bronchi.

14
00:00:36,049 --> 00:00:38,933
These branch again and again into smaller

15
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:41,817
bronchioles, and every tiny branch ends in

16
00:00:41,817 --> 00:00:43,615
air sacs called alveoli.

17
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:45,807
That's where the real work happens!

18
00:00:47,567 --> 00:00:49,781
How big is this exchange surface?

19
00:00:49,781 --> 00:00:53,115
Remarkably big! The lungs hold about 300

20
00:00:53,115 --> 00:00:56,043
million alveoli. Spread flat, they would

21
00:00:56,043 --> 00:00:58,500
cover about 70 square metres, a huge

22
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,162
surface for gas exchange packed inside

23
00:01:01,162 --> 00:01:03,873
your chest. The air arriving there is

24
00:01:03,873 --> 00:01:05,693
about 21% oxygen.

25
00:01:05,693 --> 00:01:08,407
And a healthy adult at rest refreshes it

26
00:01:08,407 --> 00:01:10,790
about 12 to 20 times a minute, every

27
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:12,131
minute of the day.

28
00:01:12,131 --> 00:01:14,949
A vast surface, a steady supply of fresh

29
00:01:14,949 --> 00:01:17,837
air: that combination is what keeps every

30
00:01:17,837 --> 00:01:19,939
cell in your body supplied.

31
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:23,401
So how does air get in?

32
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:26,092
Meet the diaphragm, a dome-shaped sheet of

33
00:01:26,092 --> 00:01:27,751
muscle under the lungs.

34
00:01:27,751 --> 00:01:30,379
When you inhale, the diaphragm contracts

35
00:01:30,379 --> 00:01:32,678
and flattens, moving down, while the

36
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:35,175
external intercostal muscles lift the

37
00:01:35,175 --> 00:01:37,876
ribs. The chest cavity gets bigger, so the

38
00:01:37,876 --> 00:01:40,469
pressure inside the lungs falls below the

39
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:43,027
air pressure outside, and air rushes in.

40
00:01:43,027 --> 00:01:45,469
During quiet breathing, the diaphragm

41
00:01:45,469 --> 00:01:48,571
alone accounts for about 75% of the air

42
00:01:48,571 --> 00:01:51,163
that enters. It is the star muscle of

43
00:01:51,163 --> 00:01:52,089
inhalation!

44
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:55,735
Now here's the elegant part.

45
00:01:55,735 --> 00:01:58,971
At rest, breathing out takes no effort at

46
00:01:58,971 --> 00:02:00,541
all: it is passive.

47
00:02:00,541 --> 00:02:02,904
When you exhale, the diaphragm and

48
00:02:02,904 --> 00:02:05,347
intercostal muscles simply relax.

49
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:07,944
The diaphragm domes upward again, and the

50
00:02:07,944 --> 00:02:10,351
elastic lungs recoil, like a stretched

51
00:02:10,351 --> 00:02:11,641
spring letting go.

52
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:13,845
The chest cavity gets smaller, the

53
00:02:13,845 --> 00:02:16,308
pressure in the lungs rises above the

54
00:02:16,308 --> 00:02:18,727
outside pressure, and air flows out.

55
00:02:18,727 --> 00:02:20,029
No pushing needed.

56
00:02:20,029 --> 00:02:21,885
Relaxation does the job!

57
00:02:23,633 --> 00:02:25,559
Let's put the two side by side!

58
00:02:25,559 --> 00:02:28,603
On the way in, the diaphragm contracts; on

59
00:02:28,603 --> 00:02:30,461
the way out, it relaxes.

60
00:02:30,461 --> 00:02:33,347
The chest cavity grows, then shrinks.

61
00:02:33,347 --> 00:02:35,949
The pressure in the lungs drops below the

62
00:02:35,949 --> 00:02:38,081
outside air, then rises above it.

63
00:02:38,081 --> 00:02:40,847
And so the air flows in, then out.

64
00:02:40,847 --> 00:02:43,286
One muscle, two states, and the pressure

65
00:02:43,286 --> 00:02:45,175
difference does the moving.

66
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:49,676
Now zoom in to a single alveolus, wrapped

67
00:02:49,676 --> 00:02:51,499
in a pulmonary capillary.

68
00:02:51,499 --> 00:02:53,941
Here, each gas moves by diffusion, from

69
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:56,511
where its partial pressure is higher to

70
00:02:56,511 --> 00:02:57,817
where it is lower.

71
00:02:57,817 --> 00:03:00,395
The alveolar wall and the capillary wall

72
00:03:00,395 --> 00:03:02,935
are each just one thin layer of cells.

73
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:05,507
Together they form the respiratory

74
00:03:05,507 --> 00:03:08,079
membrane, only about 0.5 micrometres

75
00:03:08,079 --> 00:03:10,213
thick, so diffusion is fast.

76
00:03:10,213 --> 00:03:13,003
Red blood cells arrive deoxygenated.

77
00:03:13,003 --> 00:03:16,052
Oxygen diffuses into the blood, and carbon

78
00:03:16,052 --> 00:03:19,011
dioxide diffuses out, into the air sac.

79
00:03:20,767 --> 00:03:23,488
Oxygen leaves the alveolar air, and two

80
00:03:23,488 --> 00:03:25,951
journeys begin! It passes through the

81
00:03:25,951 --> 00:03:28,369
respiratory membrane into the blood, where

82
00:03:28,369 --> 00:03:30,844
it is carried mostly by haemoglobin inside

83
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:33,255
red blood cells. The blood leaves the

84
00:03:33,255 --> 00:03:34,489
lungs oxygenated.

85
00:03:34,489 --> 00:03:37,355
Bright, oxygen-rich blood, ready for the

86
00:03:37,355 --> 00:03:40,132
whole body. Meanwhile, carbon dioxide, a

87
00:03:40,132 --> 00:03:42,928
waste product of the body's cells, makes

88
00:03:42,928 --> 00:03:44,126
the opposite trip:

89
00:03:44,126 --> 00:03:46,855
from the blood into the alveolus, and out

90
00:03:46,855 --> 00:03:48,469
with your next breath.

91
00:03:48,469 --> 00:03:51,651
In, and out. Beautifully simple!

92
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:56,793
Let's recap! Air travels down the trachea

93
00:03:56,793 --> 00:04:00,036
and bronchi to about 300 million alveoli.

94
00:04:00,036 --> 00:04:01,583
Breathing in is active:

95
00:04:01,583 --> 00:04:04,140
the diaphragm contracts and the lungs

96
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:06,859
fill. Breathing out at rest is passive:

97
00:04:06,859 --> 00:04:09,648
the muscles relax and the lungs recoil.

98
00:04:09,648 --> 00:04:12,707
And in each alveolus, oxygen diffuses into

99
00:04:12,707 --> 00:04:15,621
the blood while carbon dioxide diffuses

100
00:04:15,621 --> 00:04:18,654
out. Twelve to twenty times a minute, all

101
00:04:18,654 --> 00:04:21,608
day, all night. That's every breath you

102
00:04:21,608 --> 00:04:22,844
take, explained!

103
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,672
Keep learning with MedSkills.

